{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793794099,"nonce":"5/fUHrGhLJBb79aehxYQ/kCK8tc=","message":"Agreed by the owner 2026-11-04. Replaces the proposal in #0.\n\nScope: same repo only. Cross-repo (pub/sub or push dispatch) is not built; wait for a real case.\n\nWhy a same-repo trigger: one deploy Step makes the whole Run a Deploy Run, which turns off newest-wins cancel for the build part; and a rejected or expired deploy gate turns a passing build red. Splitting into two pipelines (ci on push, deploy on upstream) fixes both.\n\nPrerequisite (first change): every Run posts the one commit-status context \"scarab\", so two pipelines on one commit overwrite each other. Each pipeline gets its own context.\n\nTrigger:\n- Syntax: on: { upstream: { pipeline: \u003cname\u003e, status: [succeeded] } }, status defaults to succeeded; when: still applies.\n- At the upstream Run's terminal transition, an outbox message is written in the same transaction. A consumer reads .scarab/ at the upstream Run's commit and starts each Pipeline that names it. No index.\n- The downstream Run builds the upstream Run's commit (same repo).\n- Supersede key: the downstream Run inherits the upstream Run's key (scoped to its own pipeline), so a stale chain is cancelled like the CI Run that started it. Deploy Runs and Environment-governed Runs stay exempt as today.\n- A Run started by a fork pull request never triggers a follow-up.\n- Loops: each upstream-triggered Run records its chain; a pipeline already in its chain is refused, and the chain has a depth cap.\n- Delivery: at-least-once, unique key (upstream run, downstream pipeline), so a replay starts one Run.","files":null}]}